(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_zoology_humanreproduction=[{id:"neet-2026r-q140",pn:140,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Placental hormones",q:"Which of the following hormone is not secreted by human placenta?",o:["Estrogen","Progesterone","LH","hCG"],c:2,s:`The placenta acts as a temporary endocrine gland in pregnancy.
It secretes hCG, human placental lactogen (hPL), estrogen, progestogens and others.
LH is secreted by the anterior pituitary, not the placenta.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q141",pn:141,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Spermatozoa",q:"Sperm motility is due to ___________.",o:["ciliary movement","amoeboid movement","muscular movement","flagellar movement"],c:3,s:"Sperm travels through the fallopian tube by flagellar movement."},{id:"neet-2026-q141",pn:141,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Foetal development",q:`Match List I with List II related to embryonic development at various months of pregnancy:
A. The foetus movement starts and hair appears on the head \u2014 I. 24 weeks of pregnancy
B. The foetus develops limbs and digits \u2014 II. 20 weeks of pregnancy
C. The foetus develops external genital organs \u2014 III. 8 weeks of pregnancy
D. The foetus body is covered with fine hair; eyelids separate and eyelashes are formed \u2014 IV. 12 weeks of pregnancy`,o:["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],c:3,s:`By the end of the second month (8 weeks) the foetus develops limbs and digits.
By the end of 12 weeks (first trimester) most major organ systems are formed; limbs and external genital organs are well developed.
During the fifth month (20 weeks) the first movements of the foetus and hair on the head are usually seen.
By about 24 weeks (end of second trimester) the body is covered with fine hair, eyelids separate and eyelashes are formed.
So the match is A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I.
Note: Option 2 is printed as 'A-II, B-III, C-III, D-I' (C-III repeated) in the source; kept as printed. Printed answer is option 4.`},{id:"neet-2026-q143",pn:143,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Spermatogenesis",q:`Spermatogonia undergo a series of cell divisions statements to produce sperms. Select the correct from the following :
A. Spermatogonia always undergo meiotic cell division.
B. Primary spermatocytes divide mitotically to produce secondary spermatocytes.
C. Secondary spermatocytes, through their second meiotic division, produce haploid spermatids.
D. Spermatids produce spermatozoa through mitosis.
E. Spermatids transform into spermatozoa by spermiogenesis.`,o:["C and E only","A, C and E only","B, C and D only","A and E only"],c:0,s:`A is incorrect: spermatogonia undergo mitotic division.
B is incorrect: primary spermatocytes undergo the 1st meiotic division to form secondary spermatocytes.
C is correct: secondary spermatocytes undergo the 2nd meiotic division to form haploid spermatids.
D is incorrect: spermatids form spermatozoa by a differentiation process called spermiogenesis.
E is correct: spermatids produce spermatozoa through spermiogenesis.
So C and E only are correct.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q158",pn:158,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Ovulation, oogenesis",q:"The covering of ovum at ovulation is __________.",o:["zona radiata","zona pellucida","chorion","endometrium"],c:1,s:`The Graafian follicle ruptures and releases the secondary oocyte (ovum) from the ovary. This is ovulation.
The secondary oocyte has a membrane around it called zona pellucida.
Endometrium is the innermost layer of the uterine wall.
Chorion is the outermost extraembryonic membrane around the embryo.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q160",pn:160,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Male reproductive system",q:"Which of the following structure is not a part of the male reproductive system?",o:["Epididymis","Vasa efferentia","Infundibulum","Rete testis"],c:2,s:`Male accessory ducts include rete testis, vasa efferentia, epididymis and vas deferens.
Oviducts, uterus and vagina are the female accessory ducts.
Infundibulum is a part of the oviduct.`},{id:"neet-2026-q161",pn:161,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Structure around the ovum",q:`Arrange the following cell layers/structures around the female gamete, from outer to inner side:
A. Zona pellucida
B. Perivitelline space
C. Corona radiata
D. Plasma membrane of ovum`,o:["C, A, D, B","C, A, B, D","D, B, A, C","A, C, B, D"],c:1,s:`From outer to inner side the layers around the female gamete are: corona radiata \u2192 zona pellucida \u2192 perivitelline space \u2192 plasma membrane of ovum.
So the answer is C, A, B, D.`,fig:{w:300,h:232,alt:"Ovum surrounded by perivitelline space, zona pellucida and cells of the corona radiata, with sperms approaching.",d:[["c",204,125,13,"f"],["c",198.5,151,13,"f"],["c",182.8,172.6,13,"f"],["c",159.8,185.9,13,"f"],["c",133.3,188.6,13,"f"],["c",108,180.4,13,"f"],["c",88.2,162.6,13,"f"],["c",77.4,138.3,13,"f"],["c",77.4,111.7,13,"f"],["c",88.2,87.4,13,"f"],["c",108,69.6,13,"f"],["c",133.3,61.4,13,"f"],["c",159.8,64.1,13,"f"],["c",182.8,77.4,13,"f"],["c",198.5,99,13,"f"],["c",140,125,48,"B"],["c",140,125,39,""],["c",140,125,34,"f"],["t",140,129,"Ovum","m"],["p","M118 8 Q108 40 128 60 Q132 66 133 72",""],["p","M168 6 Q160 30 168 48 Q172 58 166 70",""],["p","M206 18 Q190 40 178 62",""],["c",134,76,3,"F"],["c",166,74,3,"F"],["c",176,66,3,"F"],["t",212,26,"Sperm",""],["l",210,22,184,40,""],["t",20,128,"Zona",""],["t",10,142,"pellucida",""],["l",50,124,92,123,""],["t",224,110,"Cells of the","s"],["t",224,122,"corona radiata","s"],["l",222,116,208,117,""],["l",222,122,198,139,""],["l",140,159,140,208,""],["t",140,222,"Perivitelline space","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2026r-q169",pn:169,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Menstrual cycle",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: Ovulation is caused by LH surge leading to rupture of Graafian follicles.
Statement II: Graafian follicle remaining after ovulation transform into corpus luteum and secretes large amount of estrogen.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:1,s:`A rapid rise of LH to its maximum in mid-cycle is the LH surge.
It makes the Graafian follicle rupture and release the ovum (ovulation). So Statement I is correct.
The rest of the follicle becomes the corpus luteum.
The corpus luteum secretes large amounts of progesterone, not estrogen. So Statement II is incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2025-q110",pn:110,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Sperm structure",q:`Match List - I with List - II.
List - I: A. Head; B. Middle piece; C. Acrosome; D. Tail
List - II: (i) Enzymes; (ii) Sperm motility; (iii) Energy; (iv) Genetic material`,o:["A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV"],c:0,s:`The sperm head has an elongated nucleus with the genetic material.
The middle piece has many mitochondria, which produce energy for movement.
The acrosome is a cap-like structure filled with enzymes that help in fertilisation of the ovum.
The tail of the sperm helps sperm motility, needed for fertilisation.`},{id:"neet-2025-q123",pn:123,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Pregnancy and foetal development",q:`Find the correct statement :
(A) In human pregnancy, the major organ systems are formed at the end of 12 weeks.
(B) In human pregnancy the major organ systems are formed at the end of 8 weeks.
(C) In human pregnancy heart is formed after one month of gestation.
(D) In human pregnancy, limbs and digits develop by the end of second month.
(E) In human pregnancy the appearance of hair is usually observed in the fifth month.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A and E only","B and C only","B, C, D and E only","A, C, D and E only"],c:3,s:`In a human female's pregnancy:
By the end of 12 weeks (1st trimester), most major organ systems are formed (not by the end of 8 weeks).
After one month of pregnancy, the embryo's heart is formed.
By the end of the second month, the foetus develops limbs and digits.
The first movements of the foetus and appearance of hair on the head are usually seen in the fifth month.`},{id:"neet-2025-q138",pn:138,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Gametogenesis and menstrual cycle",q:`Consider the following :
A. The reductive division for the human female gametogenesis starts earlier than that of the male gametogenesis.
B. The gap between the first meiotic division and the second meiotic division is much shorter for males compared to females.
C. The first polar body is associated with the formation of the primary oocyte.
D. Luteinizing Hormone (LH) surge leads to disintegration of the endometrium and onset of menstrual bleeding.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A and B are true","A and C are true","B and D are true","B and C are true"],c:0,s:`Statements A and B are true; C and D are false.
The first polar body is associated with the formation of the secondary oocyte (not the primary oocyte).
LH surge leads to ovulation.
Decreased progesterone in the late luteal phase leads to degeneration of the endometrium and onset of menstrual bleeding.`},{id:"neet-2025-q147",pn:147,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Menstrual cycle",q:"The first menstruation is called :",o:["Menopause","Menarche","Diapause","Ovulation"],c:1,s:`The first menstruation begins at puberty and is called menarche.
Ovulation is the release of the secondary oocyte from the mature Graafian follicle.
In human beings menstrual cycles cease around 50 years of age; this is menopause.
Diapause is a state of dormancy or developmental arrest in an organism.`},{id:"neet-2025-q176",pn:176,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Twins",q:"Twins are born to a family that lives next door to you. The twins are a boy and a girl. Which of the following must be true?",o:["They are monozygotic twins.","They are fraternal twins.","They were conceived through in vitro fertilization.","They have 75% identical genetic content."],c:1,s:`Fraternal (dizygotic) twins come from 2 separate fertilized eggs. They usually have 2 separate amniotic sacs, placentas and supporting structures.
If the twins are a boy and a girl, they are fraternal twins.`},{id:"neet-2024-q153",pn:153,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Female reproductive system (hymen)",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I: The presence or absence of hymen is not a reliable indicator of virginity.
Statement II : The hymen is torn during the first coitus only.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],c:0,s:`The hymen can also tear from a sudden jolt, use of a vaginal tampon, or active sports.
In some women the hymen stays even after coitus.
So Statement I is true and Statement II is false.`},{id:"neet-2024-q181",pn:181,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Lactation and colostrum",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R:
Assertion A : Breast-feeding during initial period of infant growth is recommended by doctors for bringing a healthy baby.
Reason R : Colostrum contains several antibodies absolutely essential to develop resistance for the new born baby.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["A is correct but R is not correct.","A is not correct but R is correct.","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."],c:2,s:`Doctors recommend breast-feeding in the early period for a healthy baby.
The reason is that colostrum has many antibodies that the newborn needs to build resistance.
So both are correct and R explains A.`},{id:"neet-2024-q184",pn:184,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Female reproductive system",q:"Which of the following is not a component of Fallopian tube?",o:["Infundibulum","Ampulla","Uterine fundus","Isthmus"],c:2,s:`Uterine fundus is the upper dome-shaped part of the uterus, above the openings of the fallopian tubes, so it is not part of the tube.
Infundibulum is the part of the oviduct nearest the ovary.
Ampulla is the wider part of the oviduct.
Isthmus is the last narrow part that joins the uterus.`},{id:"neet-2024-q189",pn:189,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Hormonal control of spermatogenesis",q:"Identify the correct option (A), (B), (C), (D) with respect to spermatogenesis.",o:["FSH, Sertoli cells, Leydig cells, spermatogenesis.","ICSH, Leydig cells, Sertoli cells, spermatogenesis","FSH, Leydig cells, Sertoli cells, spermiogenesis","ICSH, Interstitial cells, Leydig cells, spermiogenesis"],c:2,s:`GnRH acts on the anterior pituitary, which releases LH and FSH.
LH acts on Leydig cells, which make androgens; androgens help formation of spermatids.
FSH acts on Sertoli cells, which produce factors that help spermiogenesis.
So A = FSH, B = Leydig cells, C = Sertoli cells, D = spermiogenesis.`,fig:{w:400,h:184,alt:"Flow chart: GnRH acts down two branches. Left: LH, B, Androgens, Formation of spermatids. Right: (A), (C), Factors, (D).",d:[["t",197,12,"GnRH","m"],["l",82,32,310,32],["l",82,32,82,46],["l",310,32,310,46],["t",82,60,"LH","m"],["l",82,68,82,83,"a"],["t",82,97,"B","m"],["l",82,105,82,120,"a"],["t",82,134,"Androgens","m"],["l",82,142,82,157,"a"],["t",82,171,"Formation of spermatids","m"],["t",310,60,"(A)","m"],["l",310,68,310,83,"a"],["t",310,97,"(C)","m"],["l",310,105,310,120,"a"],["t",310,134,"Factors","m"],["l",310,142,310,157,"a"],["t",310,171,"(D)","m"]]},sfig:{w:400,h:184,alt:"Filled flow chart: GnRH acts on both branches. Left: LH, Leydig cell, Androgens, Formation of spermatids. Right: FSH, Sertoli cell, Factors, Spermiogenesis.",d:[["t",197,12,"GnRH","m"],["l",82,32,310,32],["l",82,32,82,46],["l",310,32,310,46],["t",82,60,"LH","m"],["l",82,68,82,83,"a"],["t",82,97,"Leydig cell","m"],["l",82,105,82,120,"a"],["t",82,134,"Androgens","m"],["l",82,142,82,157,"a"],["t",82,171,"Formation of spermatids","m"],["t",330,60,"FSH","m"],["l",330,68,330,83,"a"],["t",330,97,"Sertoli cell","m"],["l",330,105,330,120,"a"],["t",330,134,"Factors","m"],["l",330,142,330,157,"a"],["t",330,171,"Spermiogenesis","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2023-q162",pn:162,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Menstrual cycle",q:`Which of the following statement are correct regarding female reproductive cycle?
(A) In non-primate mammals cyclical changes during reproduction are called oestrus cycle.
(B) First menstrual cycle begins at puberty and is called menopause.
(C) Lack of menstruation may be indicative of pregnancy.
(D) Cyclic menstruation extends between menarche and menopause.
Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["A, C and D only","A and D only","A and B only","A, B and C only"],c:0,s:`The first menstrual cycle at puberty is called menarche, not menopause, so B is wrong.
Cyclic menstruation lasts from menarche to menopause (D true).
Absence of menstruation can indicate pregnancy (C true).
Non-primate mammals show the oestrus cycle (A true).
So A, C and D are correct.`},{id:"neet-2023-q170",pn:170,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Male and female reproductive ducts",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: Vas deferens receives a duct from seminal vesicle and opens into urethra as the ejaculatory duct.
Statement II: The cavity of the cervix is called cervical canal which along with vagina forms birth canal.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is false."],c:1,s:`The vas deferens is joined by a duct from the seminal vesicle and opens into the urethra as the ejaculatory duct, so Statement I is true.
The cervical canal together with the vagina forms the birth canal, so Statement II is true.`},{id:"neet-2023-q182",pn:182,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Menstrual cycle and implantation",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A: Endometrium is necessary for implantation of blastocyst.
Reason R: In the absence of fertilization, the corpus luteum degenerates that causes disintegration of endometrium.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A is false but R is true.","Both A and R are true and R is the correct expiation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false."],c:2,s:`Implantation is the embedding of the blastocyst in the endometrium, so A is true.
Without fertilisation the corpus luteum degenerates, progesterone falls and the endometrium breaks down (menstruation). So R is true.
But R does not explain why the endometrium is needed for implantation.`},{id:"neet-2022-q154",pn:154,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Spermatogenesis and spermiation",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I : The release of sperms into the seminiferous tubules is called spermiation.
Statement II : Spermiogenesis is the process of formation of sperms from spermatogonia.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:1,s:`Spermiogenesis is the change of spermatids into sperms, not the formation of sperms from spermatogonia (that is spermatogenesis).
So Statement II is incorrect.
Afterwards the sperm heads sit embedded in Sertoli cells and are finally released from the seminiferous tubules. This release is called spermiation.
So Statement I is correct.`},{id:"neet-2022-q162",pn:162,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Spermatogenesis versus oogenesis",q:`Which of the following statements are true for spermatogenesis but do not hold true for Oogenesis?
(a) It results in the formation of haploid gametes
(b) Differentiation of gamete occurs after the completion of meiosis
(c) Meiosis occurs continuously in a mitotically dividing stem cell population
(d) It is controlled by the Luteinising hormone (LH) and Follicle Stimulating Hormone (FSH) secreted by the anterior pituitary
(e) It is initiated at puberty
Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["(b) and (c) only","(b), (d) and (e) only","(b), (c) and (e) only","(c) and (e) only"],c:2,s:`(a) is true for both, since both make haploid gametes.
(b) is true only for spermatogenesis: spermatids change into sperms (spermiogenesis) after meiosis.
(d) is true for both, as LH and FSH control both.
(e) is true only for spermatogenesis, which is continuous from puberty. Oogenesis begins in the embryo.
(c) also fits only spermatogenesis, so the answer is (b), (c) and (e).`},{id:"neet-2022r-q168",pn:168,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Spermatogenesis",q:"How many secondary spermatocytes are required to form 400 million spermatozoa?",o:["400 million","50 million","100 million","200 million"],c:3,s:`A secondary spermatocyte divides by meiosis II into two spermatids.
The two spermatids change into two spermatozoa.
So spermatozoa are twice the number of secondary spermatocytes.
400 million \xF7 2 = 200 million.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q170",pn:170,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Mammary gland structure",q:`Arrange the components of mammary gland. (from proximal to distal).
      (a) Mammary duct
      (b) Lactiferous duct
      (c) Alveoli (d) Mammary ampulla
      (e) Mammary tubules
      Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below .`,o:["(e) \u2192 (c) \u2192 (d) \u2192 (b) \u2192 (a)","(c) \u2192 (a) \u2192 (d) \u2192 (e) \u2192 (b)","(b) \u2192 (c) \u2192 (e) \u2192 (d) \u2192 (a)","(c) \u2192 (e) \u2192 (a) \u2192 (d) \u2192 (b)"],c:3,s:`The order is alveoli, then mammary tubules, then mammary duct, then mammary ampulla, then lactiferous duct.
This is (c) \u2192 (e) \u2192 (a) \u2192 (d) \u2192 (b).`},{id:"neet-2022-q173",pn:173,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Oogenesis",q:"At which stage of life the oogenesis process is initiated?",o:["Embryonic development stage","Birth","Adult","Puberty"],c:0,s:`Oogenesis starts during embryonic development, when a couple of million oogonia form in each foetal ovary.
No oogonia are added after birth.
By puberty only 60,000 to 80,000 primary follicles remain in each ovary, as the rest degenerate.`}]})();
